# scan-secrets.sh allowlist — extended-regex, one pattern per line (# comments, blanks ignored).
# A finding whose match line matches any pattern here is suppressed. Add your org's known-safe
# values: a vendor sandbox key, a public support address, a documentation IP. Keep entries tight.

# Bot / placeholder emails used throughout this template
noreply@anthropic\.com
# Maintainer's published security-contact address (SECURITY.md). Deliberately public so
# vulnerability reports reach a human; allowlisted so the PII gate does not flag it.
m\.aslaner@outlook\.com
[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@users\.noreply\.github\.com
[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@(example|test)\.(com|org|net)
api\.example\.com

# Loopback / unspecified / RFC 5737 documentation IP ranges (not real hosts)
\b127\.0\.0\.1\b
\b0\.0\.0\.0\b
\b192\.0\.2\.[0-9]{1,3}\b
\b198\.51\.100\.[0-9]{1,3}\b
\b203\.0\.113\.[0-9]{1,3}\b

# A governed repo's vault records its own execution-ledger key in exactly one place: the
# `jira_key:` frontmatter field of an intent note (vault-as-source-of-truth, law_version 1.2.0).
# Field-scoped on purpose — prose citations still describe the run, not the ticket. NOTE: patterns
# here are matched against grep's `path:lineno:content` output, so a '^' anchor never matches;
# tie a field pattern to the leading ':' delimiter instead.
:jira_key: [A-Z][A-Z0-9]+-[0-9]+$
